Actually, let me look at the `ps_io_fgetchars` function's handling of the `eol` pointer:

```c
eol = FD_BUF + FD_LINE_END;
while (eol < tmp && *eol != '\n' && *eol != '\r') eol++;
if (eol >= tmp) eol = NULL;
if (eol) {
    if (*eol=='\r' && *(eol+1)=='\n') eol += 2;
    else eol++;
    break;
}
```

If `eol` points to a '\n' or '\r', it's incremented past it. Then:

```c
FD_LINE_END = eol - FD_BUF;
FD_LINE_LEN = FD_LINE_END - FD_LINE_BEGIN;
FD_LINE_TERMCHAR = FD_BUF[FD_LINE_END];
FD_BUF[FD_LINE_END] = '\0';
```

`FD_LINE_TERMCHAR` is set to the character at `FD_BUF[FD_LINE_END]`, which is the character after the newline. This could be uninitialized if `FD_LINE_END` is at the end of the buffer (i.e., `FD_BUF_END`). But `FD_BUF[FD_BUF_END]` was set to '\0' by the previous call, so it's initialized.

Wait, but what if `eol` points to `FD_BUF + FD_BUF_END`? This happens when the line is artificially broken:

```c
if (FD_BUF_END - FD_LINE_BEGIN > BREAK_PS_IO_FGETCHARS_BUF_SIZE) {
    eol = FD_BUF + FD_BUF_END - 1;
    break;
}
```

In this case, `eol = FD_BUF + FD_BUF_END - 1`, so `FD_LINE_END = FD_BUF_END - 1`, and `FD_LINE_TERMCHAR = FD_BUF[FD_BUF_END - 1]`. This is the last byte read from the file, which is initialized.

